{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792854956,"nonce":"eQ9xD5U/nD+cJU8HYyDB4zL/WHc=","title":"Python: reversed(range(...)) builds a list, and slices grow element by element","message":"In bm_ai's permutation loop every pass allocates a list for `reversed(range(r))` (zb_list_range and a reversed copy) only to count down it, and every slice (`indices[:r]`, `indices[i + 1:]`, `indices[i:i + 1]`) is built by pushing its elements one at a time into an empty list, reallocating as it grows; concat reserves for its first operand and then grows again for the second. A hand-typed version of the kernel spends about half of its program thread in zyntax_alloc, zyntax_free and zyntax_realloc.\n\nDone: `for x in reversed(range(a, b))` (one or two bounds) lowers as a descending counted loop over b - 1 .. a, each bound read once and in order; a unit-step slice copies its run with one memcpy through a new builtin list method `append_range(ys, lo, hi)`; concat reserves both operands' length once. NUC sweep against b664e03c (loaded box, 9 interleaved reps): fannkuch 59.3 to 49.5 ms, go 23.0 to 18.4, pyflate-fast 225.4 to 210.5, spectral-norm 14.4 to 11.8, others within noise.\n\nStill owed for ai: a comprehension over a slice could read the source in place when its element cannot run code, and a comprehension over a sized source could reserve its result; the larger gap is that its generators yield boxed values and its tuples are dynamic (see the ai profile notes).","files":null}]}